{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. Raw HTML means an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes; text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: `requirements.references.markdown_literals` states only observable parsing behavior. Escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST stay literal and MUST NOT count as references. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement. The text also defines what raw HTML means (an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes).\n\nI checked each clause for a structural obligation by asking whether a different build could give the same results and still violate it. None does. The text names no library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or interface that the implementation must use or delegate to. A hand-written scanner, a different Markdown parser, or a regex pre-pass would all conform if they gave the same literal-versus-reference classification. The choice of mechanism is discretionary.\n\nThe requirement has no implementation edges, and the review roots are empty. No implementation root is needed, because there is no structural obligation to map. Tests cover the behavioral obligations, and test adequacy is outside this assessment.\n\nbehavioral: The marked tests cover every obligation in `requirements.references.markdown_literals`.\n\n**Escaped roles stay literal and are not references.**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal]` renders a guide chapter. It asserts that the escaped, inline-code and fenced markers are all preserved and that no `habeas-req-ref-impl` badge appears.\n- The same test checks that an escaped backslash does not escape the role: with `\\\\{impl}` and `\\\\\\{impl}`, exactly one role is found, at the right offset.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal]` covers escaped roles, inline code, fenced code and an HTML attribute for `.md`, `.markdown`, `.MD` and `.Markdown` files. It asserts that no references are found. A plain `.txt` file with the same text does yield references, so the literal handling is specific to Markdown.\n- `src/terms.rs#mod[tests]/fn[term_dependencies_of_every_markdown_extension_skip_literal_roles]` and `tests/definitions.rs#fn[literal_term_roles_in_subject_and_requirements_inputs_need_no_definition]` cover the same literal contexts for `{term}` roles. The second test also checks that the same role written as a real reference does need a definition.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[citation_metadata_preserves_prose_other_roles_and_literal_contexts]` covers escaped roles, inline code, backtick and tilde fences, and raw HTML blocks, comments and tags for fenced citations.\n\n**Role-looking text in inline code, fenced code or raw HTML stays literal.**\n- Inline code and fenced code are covered by the tests above.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` covers all three raw HTML forms: an HTML block (`<div>`), an HTML comment, and an inline tag attribute (`<span title=...>`). It checks both `reference_roles` and the rendered book output.\n- `src/fenced.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_fences_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` covers colon fences inside an HTML block, a comment and a tag attribute. It contrasts Markdown with plain text.\n\n**Text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown.** `roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal` asserts that the role in `<em>{marker}</em>` is found as a reference. It also asserts that the rendered output contains a badge in `<em><a class=\"habeas-req-badge`.\n\n**A `{req}` directive in a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role do not define a requirement.**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal]` has `:::{req} block` inside a fence, an inline-code `{req}`, and a legacy `{req}`legacy``. It asserts that all three stay literal, and that the real `:::{req} visible` directive is rendered as a definition.\n- `colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal` asserts that the legacy role is not defined.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown]` shows that a fenced reference inside a requirement body is not counted.\n- `definition_blocks` is the shared parser used by the index, evidence and rendering paths, so the rendering-level assertions cover the definition obligation.\n\n`src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs]` adds an end-to-end check that inline-code and fenced roles in notes do not require evidence coverage.\n\nI found no obligation without an exercising test and an assertion on its outcome.",
